        "content": "<p>faysou opened <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14985\">PR #14985</a> from <code>faysou:cranelift-object-macho-shared-custom-sections</code> to <code>bytecodealliance:main</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>Problem</h2>\n<p><code>ObjectModule::define_data</code> calls <code>add_section</code> for every data object that has a custom section. A Mach-O object holds at most 255 sections, so <code>ObjectProduct::emit</code> fails with <code>Too many sections: 0x12d</code> once a module defines more data objects than that.</p>\n<p>This happens in practice with rustc_codegen_cranelift on aarch64 macOS: every <code>inventory::submit!</code> static goes into <code>__DATA,__mod_init_func</code>, and building <code>nautilus-model</code> hits the limit.</p>\n<h2>Change</h2>\n<p>On Mach-O, reuse an existing section when segment, name, <code>SectionKind</code>, and Mach-O flags all match, as LLVM does when it merges sections with the same name. ELF and COFF output is unchanged.</p>\n<h2>Testing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>New <code>macho_data_objects_share_custom_section</code> in <code>cranelift/object/tests/basic.rs</code>: defines 300 objects in <code>__DATA,__mod_init_func,mod_init_funcs</code> plus one in a second custom section, then checks there is exactly one section of each with the expected size and section type. It fails without the fix (<code>Too many sections: 0x12d</code>) and passes with it.</li>\n<li><code>cargo test -p cranelift-object</code>, <code>cargo fmt --check</code>, and <code>cargo clippy -p cranelift-object --all-targets</code> pass.<br>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 634735807,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792921533
